{%- render 'glp1-assets' -%}
<div class="g1 g1-soft">
  <section class="g1-sec">
    <div class="g1-narrow g1-faq">
      <div class="g1-center"><h2 class="g1-h2">Questions, answered.</h2></div>
      <div style="margin-top:18px">
        <details><summary>Can [BRAND] be taken with Ozempic, Wegovy, Mounjaro, or Zepbound?</summary><p><span class="g1-gap">[GAP: GLP-1 interaction and safety statement]</span> Spermidine is a polyamine people already eat every day, about 7 to 12 mg from food.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Biotin did nothing. Why would this be different?</summary><p>Biotin only fixes a biotin shortfall. Most women with this shed have normal labs. Their follicles weren't short on materials. They were switched to rest. Spermidine was tested on exactly that: how many hairs stay in the growth phase.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Won't the hair just grow back on its own?</summary><p>A lot of it will, eventually. Full thickness takes 12 to 18 months. And as long as the shot keeps appetite low, the famine signal keeps firing. That's why so many women get a 2nd wave. [BRAND] works on keeping follicles growing while the trigger is still there.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does quitting the shot fix it?</summary><p>No. Many women quit and keep shedding anyway, because the hairs that fell were switched to rest months earlier. Quitting doesn't flip them back.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Why not minoxidil?</summary><p>It works for some women. It also means a prescription or a greasy scalp, a "dread shed" at the start, and a habit for life. [BRAND] works on a different lever: it keeps the follicle in the growth phase, with no dread shed.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Isn't this just another vitamin?</summary><p>No. Spermidine is a polyamine, a molecule every cell makes. It's the only raw material cells can use to switch on eIF5A. No vitamin does that.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is GLP-1 hair loss permanent?</summary><p>Most GLP-1 shedding is telogen effluvium, and it's temporary. A smaller group, mostly people with a family history, see pattern hair loss show up. That's a hormone problem, and a different conversation.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How soon does it work?</summary><p>Hair runs on a 3-month clock. Hairs already at rest on day 1 still fall in the 1st weeks. The trial measured at 90 days and again at 6 months. The fair test is 3 months.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does it work for men?</summary><p>Yes. The trial had more men than women. Men on a GLP-1 get the Famine Shed far less often. Loss at the temples or crown is more likely pattern loss.</p></details>
        <details><summary>About the name.</summary><p>Yes, it was 1st found in semen. That's where the name comes from. Today it comes from <span class="g1-gap">[GAP: source]</span>. It's in every cell in the human body.</p></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

